<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Provisional Government of the French Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1944 CE – 1946 CE · capital Paris</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The government that restored the Republic after liberation, gave French women the vote and built the post-war welfare state.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In June 1944 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-committee-of-national-liberation">French Committee of National Liberation</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> renamed itself the Provisional Government of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-fifth-republic">French Republic</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> was liberated in August 1944, it moved to the capital and declared that <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vichy-france">Vichy France</a> had never been legitimate.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The government included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaullism">Gaullists</a>, communists, socialists and Christian democrats from the Resistance. Women voted for the first time in 1945. It nationalised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renault">Renault</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, electricity and major banks, created the social security system and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monnet-plan">Monnet Plan</a> for reconstruction, and oversaw the trials of collaborators. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> gained a seat on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-security-council">UN Security Council</a> and an occupation zone in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">De Gaulle</a> resigned in January 1946 over disputes about the constitution, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-gouin">Félix Gouin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-bidault">Georges Bidault</a> led the government. After a first draft was rejected, voters approved a new constitution in October 1946, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-fourth-republic">French Fourth Republic</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/provisional-government-of-the-french-republic.jpg" alt="Provisional Government of the French Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1944 CE – 1946 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Paris</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Charles de Gaulle, Félix Gouin, Georges Bidault</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>GPRF</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1945&amp;focus=provisional-government-of-the-french-republic" aria-label="Provisional Government of the French Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4734 472 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Provisional Government of the French Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5054 728L4997 651M5054 728L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1945&amp;focus=provisional-government-of-the-french-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
